# ── Mailbird website-enquiry capture ─────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env for local work. In production set these in the Vercel
# project (Settings → Environment Variables).
#
# IMPORTANT: VITE_* values are baked in at BUILD time, not read at
# runtime. Setting them in Vercel does nothing until you redeploy.

# Base URL the form posts to:
#   {VITE_MAILBIRD_URL}/api/method/mailbird.api.send_website_enquiry
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY unless you know you want to change it. The default is
# the same-origin proxy path /mailbird, which works in both environments
# with no CORS involved:
#   - locally  → Vite dev proxy      (server.proxy in vite.config.js)
#   - on Vercel → rewrite            (rewrites[0] in vercel.json)
#
# Set it to https://mailbird.techbird.in to bypass the proxy and call the
# host directly. Only do that after allow_cors is configured there - see
# the CORS section below for why direct is the better end state.
VITE_MAILBIRD_URL=

# Must match an ACTIVE Website Master domain_name on the Mailbird
# instance - not this site's URL, just the bare domain. A mismatch makes
# every enquiry fail with "Website not found or inactive."
# Defaults to techbird.in.
VITE_MAILBIRD_SITE=

# Cloudflare Turnstile sitekey. Required only if the captcha type in
# Mailbird Settings is set to Turnstile - in that case the server rejects
# every enquiry that arrives without cf_turnstile_response.
# Leave empty when no captcha is configured; the widget is then not
# rendered and no token is sent.
VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# ── CORS: the one thing that cannot be fixed in this repo ────────
# mailbird.techbird.in currently returns NO Access-Control-Allow-Origin
# header, on either the preflight or the POST. A browser will block the
# response even though the POST itself goes through, so the form falls
# back to the mailto: handoff for every visitor.
#
# The proxy above avoids this entirely: the browser only ever talks to
# this site's own origin, so there is no cross-origin request to block.
# It is wired at the same path in both environments -
# server.proxy in vite.config.js locally, rewrites[0] in vercel.json on
# Vercel - so no environment variable is needed for it to work.
#
# PROXY TRADE-OFF - the endpoint is rate limited to 5 requests per minute
# PER IP. Proxied, the Mailbird host sees Vercel's egress IP rather than
# the visitor's, unless its nginx is configured to trust the forwarded
# client IP (set_real_ip_from + real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For). Without
# that, the 5/min budget is shared across all visitors and one bad actor
# can lock the form for everyone.
#
# NGINX HOSTS - www.techbird.in is served by nginx on our own box, not by
# Vercel, so the vercel.json rewrite does not apply there. That host needs an
# equivalent proxy or the browser hits CORS and every submission falls back to
# the mailto: handoff:
#
#   location /mailbird/ {
#       proxy_pass         https://mailbird.techbird.in/;
#       proxy_set_header   Host mailbird.techbird.in;
#       proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
#       proxy_ssl_server_name on;
#   }
#
# Remember the build also happens on that box, so the VITE_* values above have
# to exist there before `npm run build` - they are baked in, not read at runtime.
#
# BETTER END STATE - allow the origin on the Mailbird host, then set
# VITE_MAILBIRD_URL=https://mailbird.techbird.in to go direct and get
# per-visitor rate limiting back. The form posts
# application/x-www-form-urlencoded, a CORS-safelisted content type, so
# no OPTIONS preflight is made and only the POST response needs the
# header. In Frappe, site_config.json:
#     "allow_cors": ["https://www.techbird.in", "https://techbird.in"]
# then `bench restart`.

# ── Go-live checklist ────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Confirm the Website Master for VITE_MAILBIRD_SITE exists and is
#    active on the Mailbird instance.
# 2. Deploy. The proxy needs no environment variables - defaults work.
# 3. If a captcha is enabled, set VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY to the matching
#    sitekey and redeploy.
# 4. Submit a real test enquiry and confirm it arrives. On failure the
#    visitor sees an error plus the fallback email; the server's actual
#    error message goes to the browser console.
# 5. Longer term, do the allow_cors change above and switch to direct.
